films.threejs_use:
three.js carries the whole world; p5.brush and Canvas2D carry only ink marks and lettering.

**The set and cast are real 3D geometry, animated as pure functions of the frame number:**
- a sliced chip slab, optical table, posts, cable and objective lens;
- 27 sphere atoms with painted face textures swapped per expression;
- the Compass: two legs worked out from a hinge each frame, and a cape made of a deforming plane;
- the Fork: grouped tines on pivots with lean and crouch;
- the Stopwatch: cylinder and torus body, capsule limbs and extruded scissor blades;
- the ribbon: planes bent per vertex along a spiral as they fly off;
- "4.7×": extruded, bevelled, hand-wobbled outlines;
- ligatures: tube arcs revealed along their length, with extruded hearts;
- AOD threads: stretched cylinders.

**Lighting and shading:**
- **Custom renderer:** a hand-authored cel ramp with a noisy terminator, warm key and cool glazes, and speculars reserved as paper white.
- **Shadows:** cast every frame from the key light, with brushy edges, drawn as engraved hatch.
- **Engraving:** burin hatching follows each form and keeps a constant spacing on screen. Brass parts are hatched along their turned surfaces, and a cross-hatch appears in deep shade.
- **Comic shading:** Ben-Day dots in the shade and a hard rim light on the shadow side.
- **Painted light:** point lights paint spill on nearby surfaces (a warm spotlight on the Stopwatch, a pink sweep before the ZAP, gold on 4.7×). Glows and beams are painted too.
- **Animated lighting:** the main light's direction and the look (pigment strength, key colour, vignette) are set every frame for the lights-down beat and the overhead ZAP flash. Purity checks still pass.

**Camera:**
- a closed-form rig per shot (orbit target, azimuth, elevation, radius, field of view, roll), blended between shots;
- a loop-safe handheld drift;
- whip pans with a smear driven by the camera's actual turn rate;
- decaying shake on hits;
- a true dolly-zoom whose radius shrinks as the field of view opens, so the subject stays the same size;
- a hero orbit for parallax on the extruded letters;
- depth of field that follows the story, drawn as painterly flattening rather than lens blur.

**Why it looks hand-made rather than default three.js:**
- nothing uses standard materials: every pixel goes through the hand-built cel ramp, hatching, dots, watercolour bleed and granulation, then boiling Sobel and inverted-hull ink with hand wobble and heavier lines on the shadow side, off-register colour plates, paper grain and ruled backdrop lines;
- all textures (faces, slab washes, sunburst cyclorama, ribbon, "Qubrio") are painted with p5.brush;
- the characters animate on twos and the ink redraws 12 times a second;
- impact frames posterise to ink and paper.

films.verification:
**Looked at frames (not just code):**
- low-res sheets every 8 frames across the whole loop, before and after the changes;
- full-res stills of key frames, including the dolly-zoom (f176, f179), ZAP (f186, f196) and finale (f296, f300);
- 1080p crops comparing the old and new look settings;
- strips around the dolly-zoom (f138–190), the opening (f10–60), the loop seam (f346–359 into f0–12, every frame) and the flash cut (f174–189, every frame);
- two review sheets pulled from the encoded master every 6 frames;
- the card-size test: 30 frames cropped and shrunk to 400×195;
- card compression quality at CRF 31 against the lossless frames (f120, f190, f300).

**Compared against:** P(doom) stills h04 and the card-size test c01, and the round-1 sheets.

**Automated checks:**
- **Purity:** frames 0/182/300/150/182/0/300/360 in shuffled order gave matching hashes, "PURITY OK" (this covers the per-frame light and look changes).
- **Render:** 1080p in four resumable ranges, one browser, 0.1–0.28 s per frame.
- **Loop check:** "f360==f0 IDENTICAL".
- **Fresh-browser re-render:** 10 random frames (213, 5, 152, 188, 99, 138, 289, 223, 82, 191), all IDENTICAL.

**Specs:**
- master: 360 frames, 15.000 s, H.264 High, yuv420p, bt709, +faststart confirmed;
- card: 360 frames, 960×528, 2.34 MB;
- poster: WebP 960×528.

**Execution rules:** every call stayed under 100 s except one card-encode attempt, which overran, went to the background and was killed; the card path was then rebuilt around a one-time 960×528 intermediate. Only one browser ran at a time. The disk guard passed before rendering, nothing was written to /tmp, and Picasso-Web was not touched.
